# 📚 Laboratory Exercise

## 📌 Exercise 11 — 1D CNN for Time-Series Forecasting

**Notebook:** `Lab_NO_11_22JZELE0480.ipynb`

### Topics Covered

- Importing TensorFlow/Keras libraries
- Loading training, validation, and testing datasets
- Defining input shape using time steps and features
- Building a 1D CNN architecture using:
  - Conv1D Layers
  - MaxPooling1D Layers
  - Dropout Layers
  - Flatten Layer
  - Dense Layers
- Compiling the CNN model
- Configuring ModelCheckpoint callbacks
- Training the model
- Monitoring training and validation performance
- Predicting future electricity demand
- Evaluating forecasting performance using regression metrics

**Learning Focus**

Develop a 1D CNN model capable of automatically extracting temporal features from sequential data for accurate electricity demand forecasting.

---

# 🔍 Core Deep Learning Concepts

## 📌 One-Dimensional Convolutional Neural Network (1D CNN)

A 1D CNN applies convolutional filters along the time dimension to identify meaningful temporal patterns within sequential data.

Unlike traditional fully connected networks, Conv1D layers automatically learn local dependencies without manual feature engineering.

---

## 📌 Sequential Feature Extraction

Convolutional filters scan across historical observations to detect recurring trends and local patterns that improve forecasting accuracy.

---

## 📌 Model Checkpointing

During training, the best-performing model is automatically saved based on validation performance using TensorFlow/Keras callbacks.

---

## 📌 Forecast Evaluation

Model performance is evaluated using several regression metrics, including:

- Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
- Mean Squared Error (MSE)
- Root Mean Squared Error (RMSE)
- Coefficient of Determination (R² Score)
- Explained Variance Score

